<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Piprahwa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Piprahwa is a village in Siddharthnagar district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>. In 1898 excavation of its stupa revealed a relic casket inscribed in Brahmi naming the relics of the Buddha and the Shakyas. Later finds of sealings bearing the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapilavastu">Kapilavastu</a> led many scholars to identify it with the Shakya capital.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/piprahwa.jpg" alt="Piprahwa" width="320" height="167" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SM7 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stupa_at_Kapilavastu,_India.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Piprahwa lies near Siddharthnagar city, 9 miles from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lumbini">Lumbini</a>, believed to be the Buddha's birthplace. According to the Mahāparinibbāna Sutta, after the Buddha's cremation around 480 BCE the Brahman Dona divided the relics among eight claimants to avoid strife, and the Buddha's own Sakya clan received a share for a stupa. The site holds a large stupa, the ruins of several monasteries and a museum, and residential complexes and shrines were uncovered at the neighbouring mound of Ganwaria.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The British engineer and landowner William Claxton Peppé noticed the mound in 1897 and set tenant farmers left in need by the famine of 1896–97 to dig it. Beneath 18 feet of brickwork his team found a stone coffer holding five small vases with bone fragments and some 1,800 gems, pearls, coral and pieces of worked gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>. Georg Bühler read the inscription as naming relics of the Buddha given by the Sakya-Sukiti brothers; John Fleet's rival reading of 1905 was rejected and he abandoned it, and scholars came to accept the translation of Auguste Barth. In 1899 the bones were handed to an emissary of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chulalongkorn">Rama V</a> of Siam and later shared among temples in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, Myanmar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>.</p><p>In the 1970s K. M. Srivastava of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archaeological-survey-of-india">Archaeological Survey of India</a> dug deeper and found two soapstone caskets with bone fragments, dated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-black-polished-ware">Northern Black Polished Ware</a> to 400-500 BCE. He concluded that the stupa was built in three phases: a mud mound 62 ft across raised by the Sakyas, an enlargement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fired-brick">fired brick</a> in the age of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>, and a squared, heightened stupa with monasteries, probably in the Kushan era. Charles Allen later examined the possibility of forgery and concluded that the forger Alois Anton Führer could not have interfered with Peppé's find.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The gold and jewellery went to the Indian Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Kolkata</a>, and Srivastava's bone fragments are in the National Museum, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a>, which has lent them for exhibitions abroad. Some scholars place ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapilavastu">Kapilavastu</a> not here but 16 kilometres to the northwest at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tilaurakot">Tilaurakot</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>. In 2025 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> stopped a planned Sotheby's auction of gems kept by the Peppé family, and its Ministry of Culture said in July that it had recovered them.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Piprahwa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the 7th or 6th century BCE they governed themselves from Kapilavastu, now identified either with Tilaurakot in Nepal or with <b>Piprahwa</b> in India.</blockquote><a href="/state/shakya-republic">Shakya republic</a></li></ul></section>
